<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Polynomials: where does the gradient take a given value (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the curve \( y = {@fx@}. \)</p>

<p>(a) Find \( \dfrac{dy}{dx} \).</p>
<div>\( \dfrac{dy}{dx} = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) Find <strong>all</strong> the values of \( x \) at which the gradient of the curve is \( {@mg@} \).</p>
<p>Enter your answer as a set of numbers in curly brackets, for example <code>{-2, 5}</code>.</p>
<div>\( x \in \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p><strong>(a)</strong> Differentiating term by term,</p>
\( \frac{dy}{dx} = {@dfx@}. \)
<p><strong>(b)</strong> The gradient is \( {@mg@} \) where \( \dfrac{dy}{dx} = {@mg@} \):</p>
\[
\begin{align*}
{@dfx@} &= {@mg@} \\
{@dfx - mg@} &= 0 \\
3\,{@factor(expand((dfx - mg)/3))@} &= 0,
\end{align*}
\]
<p>so \( x = {@rr1@} \) or \( x = {@rr2@} \). A cubic curve can have the same gradient at two different points, so both values must be given: \( x \in {@ta2@} \).</p>
<p>Check: substituting each value into \( \dfrac{dy}{dx} \) gives \( {@subst(rr1, x, dfx)@} \) and \( {@subst(rr2, x, dfx)@} \), both equal to \( {@mg@} \) as required.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2026063000</text>
